From: NanoKVM BSP <nanokvm@local>
Subject: [PATCH] riscv: dts: sophgo: nanokvm: enable i2c3 for the status OLED

The BMC drives an optional 128x64 SSD1306 status display (nanokvm-app,
pkg/device/oled) and neither existing bus can carry it: i2c1's controller is
in slave mode for the host's sensor push, and i2c4 is the HDMI bridge's bus on
1.8V-only pads. Enable i2c@4030000 on the two free SDIO1 header pads that
carry IIC3 and pin it at /dev/i2c-3.

Wiring, for the module's four pins:

	GND -> GND
	VCC -> 3V3 (not 5V: the pads are not 5V tolerant and some modules
	       pull SDA/SCL up to VCC rather than to their own regulator)
	SCL -> SD1_CMD (header P22)
	SDA -> SD1_CLK (header P23)

The pad choice, the pull configuration and the reason the node has no child
are explained inline in the DTS, because that is where anyone changing them
will be looking.

No kernel config change: I2C_DESIGNWARE_PLATFORM and I2C_CHARDEV are already
on for the other two buses.

Must follow 0012 -- it extends the aliases node 0012 adds and appends to the
same board .dts.

Not upstream -- local to this build.
---
--- a/arch/riscv/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2002-licheerv-nano-b.dts
+++ b/arch/riscv/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2002-licheerv-nano-b.dts
@@ -347,6 +347,62 @@
 / {
 	aliases {
 		i2c0 = &i2c1;
+		i2c3 = &i2c3;
 		i2c4 = &i2c4;
 	};
 };
+
+/*
+ * i2c3 -- the front-panel status display (SSD1306 OLED, 0x3c).
+ *
+ * The display needs a bus of its own because neither existing one can carry
+ * it. i2c1 runs its controller in slave mode for the host's sensor push
+ * (eeprom@50 above), and a DesignWare controller is a master or a slave, not
+ * both: it cannot address a panel at all. i2c4 is the LT6911C's bus, its pads
+ * are IO_TYPE_1V8_ONLY, and the display module pulls SDA/SCL up to its 3.3V
+ * supply -- which is not a level those pads may see.
+ *
+ * IIC3 comes out on SD1_CMD (SCL) and SD1_CLK (SDA), function 2 on both
+ * (CVITek pad table: SD1_CMD__IIC3_SCL = 2, SD1_CLK__IIC3_SDA = 2). They are
+ * the two SDIO1 pads i2c1 left over that carry an I2C function this board is
+ * not already using -- SD1_D2/SD1_D1 are only an alternative route for IIC1 --
+ * and they sit on the same header, in the same 3.3V VDDIO_SD1 domain, as the
+ * i2c1 pair. Same pad type as i2c1_cfg, so the same properties.
+ *
+ * bias-pull-down = <0> is not redundant with bias-pull-up. These pads reset
+ * with their pull-down enabled, and pinctrl-cv18xx treats the two as separate
+ * bits: asking for the pull-up alone leaves both on, a 60k/62k divider that
+ * parks an undriven line at mid-rail. With a display fitted its own pull-ups
+ * win and nobody notices; with the header empty -- the normal state of a
+ * board without the option -- the controller reads a bus that is neither idle
+ * nor busy every time userspace probes for the panel. Cleared, an empty bus
+ * idles high and the probe gets a clean NACK.
+ *
+ * The node is deliberately empty: no ssd1306 child. The panel is optional and
+ * hot-pluggable, and it is driven from userspace through i2c-dev (nanokvm-app,
+ * pkg/device/oled), which handles its absence by probing. A child node would
+ * bind a kernel driver to a device that is usually not there and take the
+ * address away from the app when it is.
+ *
+ * 400kHz is within both the controller's and the SSD1306's range and brings a
+ * full 1KiB frame down to about 25ms on the wire.
+ */
+&pinctrl {
+	i2c3_cfg: i2c3-cfg {
+		i2c3-pins {
+			pinmux = <PINMUX(PIN_SD1_CMD, 2)>,
+				 <PINMUX(PIN_SD1_CLK, 2)>;
+			bias-pull-up;
+			bias-pull-down = <0>;
+			drive-strength-microamp = <10800>;
+			power-source = <3300>;
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+&i2c3 {
+	pinctrl-0 = <&i2c3_cfg>;
+	pinctrl-names = "default";
+	clock-frequency = <400000>;
+	status = "okay";
+};
